{
  "abstract": "Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule asked which residue sets $A$ mod $2^m$ must be met by every nonconvergent Collatz trajectory; they proved the mod-4 cases and $\\{2\\},\\{3\\},\\{4\\},\\{6\\},\\{1,5\\},\\{1,7\\}$ mod 8 and left $\\{1\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{5,7\\}$ mod 8 open. We study the simplest certificate for such statements: a potential $\\log_2 x+\\Phi(x \\bmod 2^g)$ with negative drift on the non-simple strongly connected components of a residue graph, plus exclusion of periodic patterns through their rational pins. Theorem A states this certificate with its finite hypothesis (all $n<X_0$ converge) and adaptive exact bounds on $\\log_2 3$. Theorem B characterizes it: the drift condition holds if and only if every ergodic invariant measure of nonnegative drift on the avoiding 2-adic set has entropy zero. Consequently the verdict is independent of the resolution $g$, and each open class carries positive-entropy nonnegative-drift measures ($h_+=.1967$ per $C$-step for $\\{5,7\\}$, computed in closed form). Exact computations cover all sets mod 8 and $|A|\\le 3$ mod 16 and mod 32. A closure test shows that all mod-8 results and the mod-16 singletons $\\{6\\},\\{12\\}$ follow from the known results; the closure-new statements reduce to a basis of two pairs mod 16, one pair and nine triples mod 32, and one cascade statement at modulus $2^{13}$. Finally we isolate a sign barrier. Every computed failure is caused by a negative rational periodic point; supercritical pins are always negative; near the cycle of $-5$ explicit homoclinic words $H_j$ give failures at every 2-adic depth, so avoidance of $7$ mod 8 together with the $-5$ cycle is certified up to $g=13$ and fails for all $g\\ge 14$. Hence no certificate of this architecture, at any fixed resolution, settles an open class; certificates whose 2-adic resolution grows with $\\log_2 x$, and arguments outside this architecture, are not excluded. No open class is resolved.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v2 addresses the clarifications of the v1 review. No theorem, proof or computed value changed.\n(1) Scope of the sign barrier. The abstract sentence 'Any proof of an open class must therefore use positivity beyond every finite 2-adic scale' is replaced by the proved statement: no certificate of this architecture at a fixed resolution settles an open class. Certificates with resolution growing with log2 x, and arguments outside this architecture, are explicitly not excluded. The same scoping is applied in Section 1, the interpretation of Section 6 and the Limitations.\n(2) Attribution. Section 2 now cites Chang [2, Remark C.15] for invariance of the maximum cycle mean under refinement in one cylinder-averaged construction, and distinguishes it from the general statement of Corollary C. It also cites Laarhoven-de Weger [10] for dyadic Collatz graphs, de Bruijn graphs and parity conjugacy.\n(3) k1_certify.py is no longer described as deciding every input. Section 3 states its verified scope: it uses a finite convergent list and rounds delta to multiples of 1e-7, so it stops with an assertion for sufficiently small margins. No such case occurs in the reported scopes.\n(4) Small fixes. X0 = 1 when there is no non-simple SCC. The sign lemma now says that negative pins never violate (Z), and are correctly excluded in simple SCCs. The shortcut map T is defined before entropies per T-step are reported. The corrupted unit in the abstract is fixed.",
